# arsenal.pins -- INPUT integrity pins for arsenal/build-arsenal-c.sh, checked
# BEFORE anything is built. this is the opposite end of arsenal.lock: the lock
# ATTESTS the output after the build (and drifts per build for the tools that
# embed a build-id), so it can never gate the input. a post-build attestation
# cannot stop a swapped tarball -- by the time it is written the swap already
# compiled and ran. these pins can.
#
# WHY A DIGEST, NOT HTTPS, IS THE ANCHOR. same reasoning as build.sh get():
# the url only decides where the bytes arrive from, the sha256 decides whether
# they are the right bytes. so a source whose host has no working TLS (socat's
# dest-unreach.org serves a cert for another domain entirely) is still safe
# over plain http once the digest is pinned -- and the build-arsenal-c.sh
# fetch() falls back to the wayback machine's copy of the SAME url, because a
# mirror cannot make a bad tarball pass this check.
#
# git sources pin the COMMIT, which is itself a sha1 over the tree: a moved tag
# (masscan/whois/hydra/jq/rg all pin a tag, which upstream can re-point) or a
# moved branch head (frotz ships no release, so it was cloning an UNPINNED HEAD
# -- a silent new-code-every-build hole this closes) fails the rev-parse check.
#
# these digests were first observed on 2026-09-13 from each project's canonical
# host over https where the host offered a valid cert. upgrading an entry to a
# maintainer signature (nmap and tcpdump both publish one) is the follow-up;
# fetch-time enforcement is the floor this establishes.
#
# name        kind  ref                                                                     sha256 (url) | commit (git)
